WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.554
Your app wants to connect to example.com.

2
00:00:05.554 --> 00:00:08.687
But machines connect to addresses, not names.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.249
DNS turns the name into an address.

4
00:00:11.249 --> 00:00:16.081
No single server knows every name, so the question travels down a chain.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.217
First stop, the stub resolver.

6
00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:21.028
It is a small part of your operating system.

7
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:25.342
It checks the file /etc/hosts, and its own cache.

8
00:00:25.342 --> 00:00:26.516
Nothing there.

9
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:29.326
So it asks a recursive resolver.

10
00:00:29.326 --> 00:00:34.917
Your router, your internet provider, or a public one such as 1.1.1.1.

11
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:36.693
Its cache is empty too.

12
00:00:36.693 --> 00:00:39.947
So it starts at the top, and asks a root server.

13
00:00:40.167 --> 00:00:42.445
The root does not know the answer.

14
00:00:42.445 --> 00:00:45.796
Each level only knows who runs the level below it.

15
00:00:45.796 --> 00:00:48.677
So the root says: ask the servers for .com.

16
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.920
Next, a .com server, called a TLD server, for top-level domain.

17
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.425
It says: ask example.com's own name servers.

18
00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:03.571
Last, the authoritative server for example.com gives the real answer: the address.

19
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:07.924
The answer flows back to your app, and each layer on the way caches it.

20
00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:10.491
Every record has a TTL, in seconds.

21
00:01:10.491 --> 00:01:12.984
It says how long anyone may cache it.

22
00:01:12.984 --> 00:01:16.217
This one says an hour, and the clock is running.

23
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:20.757
Ask again within that hour, and the stub answers straight from its cache.

24
00:01:20.757 --> 00:01:22.356
No trip to the root at all.

25
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.616
Now say the site moves to a new address.

26
00:01:25.616 --> 00:01:28.983
The authoritative server changes its answer right away.

27
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.613
People say a DNS change takes time to propagate.

28
00:01:32.613 --> 00:01:38.301
What really happens is that old answers sit in caches, until their TTL runs out.

29
00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:42.135
So teams lower the TTL a day before a planned move.

30
00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:46.867
One hour becomes 60 seconds, so old answers expire within a minute.

31
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.918
Now the app has an address.

32
00:01:50.918 --> 00:01:56.555
The URL names no port, so curl uses 443, the HTTPS port.

33
00:01:56.767 --> 00:02:01.851
Before any data moves, TCP opens a connection with a three-step handshake.

34
00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:02.442
One.

35
00:02:02.442 --> 00:02:04.412
The client sends SYN.

36
00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:05.820
I want to talk.

37
00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:06.399
Two.

38
00:02:06.399 --> 00:02:08.871
The server answers SYN-ACK.

39
00:02:08.871 --> 00:02:10.427
Fine, I hear you.

40
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:11.086
Three.

41
00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:14.779
The client sends ACK, and the connection is open.

42
00:02:14.779 --> 00:02:17.267
Now the bytes can flow, in order.

43
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.228
But connections fail, and the error tells you which step broke.

44
00:02:22.228 --> 00:02:25.033
Say you try port 8000, where nothing listens.

45
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:29.569
The machine is there, so it answers your SYN, with a reset.

46
00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:30.793
Connection refused.

47
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.773
A refusal comes back in about a millisecond.

48
00:02:33.773 --> 00:02:37.051
The host is there, but nothing listens on that port.

49
00:02:37.967 --> 00:02:39.488
A timeout is different.

50
00:02:39.488 --> 00:02:41.010
Nothing answers at all.

51
00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.773
A firewall dropped the packets, or the address is wrong, or unreachable.

52
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.420
Your machine hears nothing back, so it sends the SYN again.

53
00:02:49.633 --> 00:02:53.907
It resends a few times, waiting longer each time, before it gives up.

54
00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:54.926
Timed out.

55
00:02:54.926 --> 00:02:57.304
So even the waiting is a clue.

56
00:02:58.333 --> 00:02:59.950
So read the error first.

57
00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:05.408
Could not resolve host means DNS failed, and no packet went to the server at all.

58
00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:07.767
Connection refused means a reset.

59
00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:11.129
The host answered, but nothing listens on that port.

60
00:03:11.333 --> 00:03:13.194
And a timeout means no answer.

61
00:03:13.194 --> 00:03:16.296
The packets were dropped, or the address is wrong.

62
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.470
First DNS turns the name into an address.

63
00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:23.020
Then TCP shakes hands with that address and port.
